  "slug": "reveals-genome-9303",
  "summary": "Three preprints release new reference genomes for a Critically Endangered skink, a Gulf reef coral and a hydrozoan jellyfish.",
  "synthesis": "The three items are de novo genome assemblies for non-model eukaryotes, each presented as a genomic resource. The Bermuda skink (Plestiodon longirostris) assembly is the first chromosome-scale reference for a Critically Endangered paleoendemic species, built from Nanopore long reads and accompanied by a mitogenome. The Persian/Arabian Gulf reef coral Cyphastrea microphthalma is reported as a 681.2 Mb assembly with 5,554 scaffolds, contig N50 244.4 kb and BUSCO completeness of 94.1%. The hydrozoan jellyfish Cladonema pacificum receives a chromosome-level assembly from HiFi and Hi-C data (504 Mb, 17,206 protein-coding genes), with phylogenomic analyses reported. The common thread is the production of contiguous, annotated references for taxa outside standard model organisms, with chromosome-level structure achieved for two of the three. Comparative downstream analyses are reported only for the jellyfish.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
